  "whyItWorks": "The ear loves hearing an idea come back. Because the rhythm and the shape stay the same, every small change stands out, and those changes are exactly the half steps that lead from one chord to the next. The rests between statements give the listener time to hear that, and give you time to plan the next one.",
  "fingeringNote": "Fifth position: index finger on fret 5, ring finger on fret 7, little finger on fret 8, one finger per fret. Everything is on the G string and the D string, with no stretches and no shifts. In bar 3 the ring finger rolls across from the D string to the G string at fret 7.",
  "practiceTask": "Play the lick over bars 1 to 4 of the weekly standard, then over bars 9 to 12 and 21 to 24, which have the same chords. Then invent your own three-note motif over {{chord 1}} and bend it to fit the next three chords, moving only the notes that clash.",
  "variation": {
    "description": "Same notes, new place in the bar: the first three statements now start on beat two instead of the 'and' of one, so their long notes land on beat three and sound calmer. The last statement still comes in on beat four and lands on the downbeat of {{chord 4}}.",
